---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import { caseStudies } from '../../lib/projects';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

const project = caseStudies[1];

const sections = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'terminal', label: 'The terminal' },
  { id: 'proxy', label: 'The proxy-attendance trap' },
  { id: 'finale', label: 'Two days before the finale' },
  { id: 'result', label: 'The result' },
];
---
<CaseLayout project={project} sections={sections} prev={{ slug: 'crynet', title: 'CryNet' }} next={{ slug: 'evoting', title: 'Biometric e-voting' }}>
  <!-- 01 overview -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="overview">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">01</span><span class="rail__label">Overview</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      AttendX is a smart biometric attendance and management system, built for the NASCOMSOFT
      EMBEDED Hackathon 2.0 over five weeks as <strong>TEAM RAMP</strong>, a four-person team. I
      ran product and worked on the hardware. The idea: clock
      in with a finger instead of a signature, and make sure nobody can clock in for somebody else.
    </p>
    <dl class="kv" data-reveal>
      <dt>Team</dt><dd>4 people; I handled product management and hardware engineering</dd>
      <dt>Timeline</dt><dd>5 weeks (Aug – Oct 2026), grand finale 10 October 2026</dd>
      <dt>Core parts</dt><dd>ESP32-S3-CAM · SFM-V1.7 capacitive fingerprint module · 16×2 I²C LCD · 4×4 keypad · 3D-printed enclosure</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 the terminal -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="terminal">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">02</span><span class="rail__label">The terminal</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The terminal walks a member through one interaction: identify on the LCD, present a finger or
      a PIN, get a verdict in the time it takes to put a hand down. Everything on the bench was
      wired and tested before the enclosure was designed around it.
    </p>
    <ul class="body bullets" data-reveal>
      <li><strong>Enrolment and matching on-module.</strong> The SFM-V1.7 capacitive sensor keeps templates inside the module itself, so the terminal works with no network and no database to lose.</li>
      <li><strong>Two clock-in paths.</strong> Fingerprint for the honest path; a 4×4 keypad PIN as the fallback for a damaged or bandaged finger. The fallback path is where the interesting engineering ended up.</li>
      <li><strong>A visible, auditable state machine.</strong> The 16×2 LCD mirrors the terminal's state at every step: waiting → identifying → matched/rejected → logged, so a failure is never silent.</li>
      <li><strong>Enclosure measured from the real loom.</strong> The camera, sensor, LCD and keypad live in a 3D-printed enclosure that was measured from the actual wiring loom, not from the schematic.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 proxy -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="proxy">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">03</span><span class="rail__label">The proxy-attendance trap</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      Every attendance system has a hole, and ours was obvious: the keypad PIN path. A friend with
      your PIN can clock you in from a car park. Removing the PIN would have punished everyone with
      a genuine fingerprint problem, so instead <strong>the fallback stops being anonymous</strong>.
    </p>
    <div class="note" data-reveal>
      <p class="note__title">Design decision</p>
      <p>The moment an employee clocks in by keypad PIN instead of fingerprint, the ESP32-S3-CAM
      fires an <strong>automatic camera snapshot</strong> and attaches it to that log entry. The PIN
      path still works for the person it was built for, but proxy attendance now leaves a face
      next to the record.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      It is the cheapest possible anti-spoofing: no extra sensor, no model to run, one wire of
      logic between "PIN accepted" and "camera captures". Designing the hole to close itself was
      the part judges remembered.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 finale -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="finale">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">04</span><span class="rail__label">Two days before the finale</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The fingerprint modules arrived late, two days before the grand finale. There was no version
      of the demo without them, so the last two days became a bring-up sprint: get an unfamiliar
      sensor talking on an unfamiliar board, on deadline.
    </p>
    <ul class="body bullets" data-reveal>
      <li><strong>UART first.</strong> Wiring, voltage levels and baud verified point to point with a multimeter and logic probing before any library was trusted; the module speaks a specific UART protocol and every symptom, from silence to garbage bytes, traced back to levels or wiring.</li>
      <li><strong>Then power.</strong> The sensor's current draw on a shared rail showed brownout-shaped behaviour during capture; separating the supply path settled it.</li>
      <li><strong>Result:</strong> a working physical demo on finale day: fingerprint clock-ins, PIN fallback with snapshot, the full loop on the enclosed terminal.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 result -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="result">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">05</span><span class="rail__label">The result</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <div class="stats" data-reveal>
      <div class="stat stat--amber"><p class="stat__value">1st place</p><p class="stat__label">Overall, NASCOMSOFT EMBEDED Hackathon 2.0 grand winner</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">4</p><p class="stat__label">Person team; product management and hardware engineering</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">2 days</p><p class="stat__label">From late sensor delivery to working demo</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">1</p><p class="stat__label">Design decision that closed the system's own hole</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      TEAM RAMP took <strong>1st place overall</strong> at the NASCOMSOFT EMBEDED Hackathon 2.0 grand
      finale. For me this project is the smallest complete example of how I like to work: treat
      the hardware as part of the product, wire it so failures are visible, and take the system's
      design hole as seriously as its feature list.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  .bullets { margin-top: 1.25rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.8rem; }
  .bullets li { padding-left: 1.1rem; position: relative; color: var(--muted); }
  .bullets li::before { content: '▪'; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--amber); }
  .bullets :global(strong) { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }
  .note { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
</style>
